WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.710
<v Narrator>So let's talk about Amazon Aurora</v>

2
00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.330
because the exam is starting to ask you

3
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.680
a lot of question about it.

4
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.810
Now, you don't need deep knowledge on it,

5
00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.910
but you need enough high-level overview

6
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.380
to understand exactly how it works,

7
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.720
so this is what I'm going to give you in this lecture.

8
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.900
Aurora is going to be a proprietary technology from AWS.

9
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.580
It's not open-sourced.

10
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.680
But they made it so it's going to be compatible

11
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.540
with PostgreSQL and MySQL.

12
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.010
And basically, your Aurora database

13
00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.420
will have compatible drivers.

14
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.860
That means that if you connect

15
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.360
as if you were connecting to your PostgreSQL

16
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.820
or a MySQL database, then it will work.

17
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.990
Aurora is very special,

18
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.970
and I won't go too deep into the internals,

19
00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.560
but they made it cloud-optimized.

20
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.560
And by doing a lot of optimization and smart stuff,

21
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.990
basically they get 5x performance improvements

22
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.520
over MySQL on the RDS,

23
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.220
or 3x extra performance of PostgreSQL on RDS.

24
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.900
Not just that, but in many different ways,

25
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.850
they also get more performance improvements.

26
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.470
To me, I watch it, it's really, really smart,

27
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.390
but I won't go into the details of it.

28
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.490
Now, Aurora storage automatically grows,

29
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.275
and I think this is one of the main features

30
00:00:55.275 --> 00:00:56.400
that is quite awesome.

31
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.690
You start at 10 gigabytes,

32
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.230
but as you put more data into your database,

33
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.620
it grows automatically up to 256 terabytes.

34
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.420
Again, this has to do with how to design it,

35
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.110
but the awesome thing is that now as a DBA or a SysOps,

36
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.720
you don't need to worry about monitoring your disk,

37
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.390
you just know it will grow automatically with time.

38
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.420
Or I can have up to 15 read replicas,

39
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.430
and the replication process

40
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.440
is going to be faster than MySQL.

41
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.100
You will see sub-10-millisecond replica lag, typically.

42
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.600
Now, if you do failover in Aurora,

43
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.520
it was going to be instantaneous.

44
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.620
So it's going to be much faster than a failover

45
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.170
from Multi-AZ on MySQL or RDS.

46
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.580
And because it's cloud-native,

47
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.670
by default, you get high availability.

48
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.740
Now, although the cost is a little bit more than RDS,

49
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.590
about 20% more, it is so much more efficient

50
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.740
that at scale, it makes a lot more sense for savings.

51
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.560
So let's talk about the aspect that are super important,

52
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.990
which is high availability and read scaling.

53
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.690
So Aurora is special because it's going to store

54
00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.600
six copies of your data anytime you write anything

55
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.340
across three AZ.

56
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.950
And so, Aurora is made such as it's available,

57
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.920
so it only needs four copies out of six for writes.

58
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.620
So that means that if one AZ is down, you're fine.

59
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.090
And it only needs you have three copies

60
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.040
out of six needed for reads.

61
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.100
So, again, that means that it's highly available for reads.

62
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.200
There is some kind of self-healing process that happens,

63
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.300
which is quite cool, which is that if some data

64
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.450
is corrupted or or bad, then it does self-healing

65
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.430
with peer-to-peer replication in the backend,

66
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.360
and it's quite cool.

67
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.280
And you don't rely on just one volume,

68
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.630
you rely on hundreds of volumes.

69
00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:29.160
Again, not something for you to manage,

70
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.420
it happens in the backend,

71
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.450
but that means that you just reduce the risk by so much.

72
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.820
So if you look at it from the diagram perspective,

73
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.670
you have three AZ and you have a shared storage volume,

74
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.110
but it's a logical volume,

75
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.730
and it does replication, self-healing,

76
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.130
and auto-expanding, which is a lot of features.

77
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.070
So if you were to write some data, maybe blue data,

78
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.310
you'll see the six copies of it in three different AZ.

79
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.780
Then if you write some orange data,

80
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.060
again, six copies of it in different AZ.

81
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.860
And then as you write more and more data,

82
00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.380
it's basically going to go, again, six copies of it

83
00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.580
in three different AZ.

84
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.440
The cool thing is that it goes on different volumes

85
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.020
and it's striped and it works really, really well.

86
00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.690
Now we need to know about storage and that's it.

87
00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.760
But you don't actually interface with the storage,

88
00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.500
it's just a design that Amazon made,

89
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.060
and I want to give it to you as well,

90
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.940
so you understand what Aurora takes.

91
00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.930
Now, Aurora is like Multi-AZ for RDS.

92
00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.390
Basically there's only one instance that takes writes,

93
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.400
so there is a master in Aurora,

94
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.990
and that's where we'll take writes.

95
00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.780
And then if the master doesn't work,

96
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.230
the failover will happen in less than 30 seconds on average.

97
00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.270
So it's really, really quick failover.

98
00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.680
And on top of the master,

99
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.590
you can have up to 15 read replicas,

100
00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.730
all serving reads.

101
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.170
So you can have a lot of them,

102
00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.210
and this is how you scale your read workload.

103
00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.090
And so any of these read replicas can become the master

104
00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.500
in case the master fails,

105
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.110
so it's quite different from how RDS works.

106
00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.810
But by default, you only have one master.

107
00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.280
The cool thing about these read replicas

108
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.190
is that it supports cross-region replication.

109
00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.870
So if you look at Aurora

110
00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.130
on the right-hand side of the diagram,

111
00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.510
this is what you should remember:

112
00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.520
One master, multiple replicas,

113
00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.730
and the storage is going to be replicated, self-healing,

114
00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.610
auto-expanding, little blocks by little blocks.

115
00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:11.820
Now, let's have a look at how Aurora is as a cluster.

116
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.430
So this is more around how Aurora works

117
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.170
when you have clients, how do you interface

118
00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.330
with all these instances?

119
00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.340
So as we said, we have a shared storage volume

120
00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.870
that is auto-expanding.

121
00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.180
Your master is the only thing that will write

122
00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.410
to your storage.

123
00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.870
And because the master can change and failover,

124
00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:31.200
what Aurora provides you is what's called a writer endpoint.

125
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.300
So it's a DNS name, a writer endpoint,

126
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.860
and it's always pointing to the master.

127
00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.720
So even if the master fails over,

128
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.940
your client still talks to the writer endpoint

129
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.180
and is automatically redirected to the right instance.

130
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.380
Now, as I said before,

131
00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.390
you also have a lot of read replicas.

132
00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.670
What I didn't say is that they can have

133
00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.740
auto-scaling on top of these read replicas.

134
00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.350
So you can have one up to 15 read replicas

135
00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:53.850
and you can set up auto-scaling,

136
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.450
such as you always have the right number of read replicas.

137
00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:58.800
Now, because you have auto-scaling,

138
00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.780
it can be really, really hard for your applications

139
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.450
to keep track of where are your read replicas,

140
00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.700
what's the URL, how do I connect to them.

141
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.350
So for it, you have to remember this

142
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.030
absolutely for going for the exam:

143
00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.400
There is something called a reader endpoint.

144
00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.470
And a reader endpoint has the exact same feature

145
00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.610
as a writer endpoint.

146
00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.440
It helps with connection load balancing,

147
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.950
and it connects automatically to all the read replicas.

148
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.340
So anytime the client connects to the reader endpoint,

149
00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.740
it will get connected to one of the read replicas,

150
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.383
and they will be load balancing done this way.

151
00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.650
Just notice that the load balancing

152
00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.330
happens at the connection level,

153
00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.770
not the statement level.

154
00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.840
So this is how it works for Aurora.

155
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.300
Remember writer endpoint, reader endpoint.

156
00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.410
Remember auto-scaling.

157
00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.810
Remember shared storage volume that auto-expands.

158
00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.880
Remember this diagram because once you get it,

159
00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.100
you'll understand how Aurora works.

160
00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.170
Now, if we go deep into the feature,

161
00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.820
you get a lot of things I already told you,

162
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.130
automatic failover, backup and recovery,

163
00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.800
isolation and security, industry compliance,

164
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.170
push-button scaling by auto-scaling.

165
00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.730
Automated patching with zero downtime,

166
00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.430
so it's kind of cool the magic they do in the backend.

167
00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.620
Advanced monitoring, routine maintenance.

168
00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.150
So all these things are handled for you.

169
00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.250
And you also get this feature called backtrack,

170
00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.290
which is giving you the ability to restore data

171
00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.820
at any point of time,

172
00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.590
and actually doesn't rely on backups,

173
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.030
it relies on something different.

174
00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:15.997
But you can always say,

175
00:06:15.997 --> 00:06:17.880
"I want to go back to yesterday at 4:00 PM."

176
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.890
And you say, "Oh, no, actually I wanted to do

177
00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.330
yesterday at 5:00 PM."

178
00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.240
And it will work as well, which is super, super neat.

179
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.350
So that's it for Aurora,

180
00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.300
and I will see you in the next lecture.

